                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the owner's position`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Treasury has flagged your Outline Business Case as weak: a <strong>BCR of 1.30</strong> against a required ≥1.80, a value proposition framed as an output, and a single £4.8bn lump-sum that concentrates risk badly. Before you fix anything, decide what your job actually is. The prior regional scheme overran 60% and opened four years late because its owner behaved as a <em>passive client</em> — signed a lump sum and hoped. Separate what only the owner can do (shape, sponsor, govern) from what the supply chain does (deliver). The trap is treating this as a costing error and shaving the estimate, when the real weakness is <em>output framing</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Shape the value proposition`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Re-articulate what Pennine Connect is <em>for</em>. A Treasury reviewer does not fund £4.8bn to lay track — they fund it to add <strong>30,000 jobs</strong>, drive agglomeration and shift freight off the M62. Move from output to a benefits-led case and rebuild the BCR toward <strong>≥1.80</strong>. The <strong>second station drives ~40% of benefits</strong> but costs £620M — cut it and you lose the catchment; below a 1.50 BCR floor it is cut for you. Decide the scope that drives value, evidence it rigorously, and name who owns the benefits after handover.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Structure the delivery model`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `This is the central commercial decision. Choose across the risk-allocation spectrum: a single lump-sum, a disaggregated multi-package model, an integrated alliance, or a target-cost (pain/gain) contract. A full lump-sum hands the entire £4.8bn and the tunnel risk to one contractor — who either prices a <em>£700M–1.1bn premium you can't afford</em>, or accepts and goes bust mid-tunnel, dropping the risk back on you. Allocate the city-centre tunnel and live-rail junction risk to the party best able to manage it, and align the supply chain with a <strong>pain/gain incentive</strong> — an alliance without one just overruns together.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Stage-gate the investment`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Convert the £4.8bn from a single sanction into a <strong>staged investment with decision gates</strong> — design, enabling works, main works release. Decide how much capital to authorize today, what evidence unlocks the next tranche, and where you keep the right to stop, reprice or descope. Commit the full £4.8bn before a single tunnel borehole and, when the interface comes in <em>£300M over</em>, you have no gate to stop at and no leverage to reprice — you just write the cheque. Lower early commitment plus hard go/no-go criteria is how staging cuts your worst-case exposure.`,
                    briefing_title_r5:      `Round 5 — Govern and defend to the sponsor board`,
                    briefing_text_r5:       `Integrate value, delivery model and investment plan, then face the Treasury/sponsor board. A <strong>live £300M tunnel cost shock</strong> hits now. Show that your structure absorbs it <em>through the contract and the gates</em> — repriced at a gate, contained in a shared risk pool — rather than landing on the public balance sheet. Define where the owner's decision rights live across the gates, or in the next crisis it gets decided by whoever shouts loudest. Defend the four owner KPIs honestly: BCR/NPV, schedule adherence, cost variance and stakeholder value.`,

