                    coach_default:          `Owner decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_investor:      `<strong>That is the owner's job.</strong> Your value is created or destroyed before and around construction — shaping what the project is for, structuring how it's contracted, and governing investment through gates. Now use that lens on everything that follows.`,
                    coach_r1_client:        `<strong>That is exactly how the last scheme overran 60%.</strong> A passive client signs a lump sum and hopes; when the tunnel goes wrong the risk lands right back on the public owner. You are an investor and value-shaper, not a buyer of an asset.`,
                    coach_r1_builder:       `<strong>You are not a builder.</strong> Your lean 140-strong team lets and governs multi-billion-pound contracts — it does not lay track. Trying to manage construction directly squanders the owner's real leverage: shaping, contracting and gating.`,
                    coach_r1_banker:        `<strong>Securing the money is the easy part.</strong> Releasing £4.8bn on a calendar, without shaping value or allocating risk, is precisely the passive-client posture that the sponsor is warning you against. The owner's work is upstream of the cash.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named your role as owner-investor AND located the weakness in output framing rather than the cost estimate. That is the lens that rebuilds the BCR — a railway funded for outcomes, not for track.`,
                    coach_r1_estimate:      `<strong>You've shaved the estimate and the BCR is still 1.31.</strong> The sponsor didn't ask for a cheaper railway — they asked what it's for. A costing tweak never moves a marginal business case. Reframe the value.`,
                    coach_r1_optimism:      `<strong>A tougher contractor doesn't fix the case.</strong> The weakness isn't who builds it — it's that the project is framed as an output, so the benefits never justify £4.8bn. Diagnose the proposition, not the bidder.`,
                    coach_r1_politics:      `<strong>Lobbying won't rescue a 1.30 BCR.</strong> Treasury's critique is technical, not political: the value proposition reads as “build the railway”. Fix the case, and the politics follow.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how the prior scheme failed.</strong> Refusing to name an owner-shaping failure means you'll trim the estimate and resubmit the same weak case. Own the front end honestly or repeat the 60% overrun.`,
                    coach_r2_outcomes:      `<strong>That is a fundable case.</strong> 30,000 jobs, agglomeration and freight off the M62 are what a Treasury reviewer funds £4.8bn for — not 38 km of track. A benefits-led proposition is how the BCR climbs toward 1.80.`,
                    coach_r2_trim:          `<strong>You've shaved 4% and the BCR barely moved.</strong> The reviewer asked what the railway is for, and you answered with a discount. Outputs don't justify £4.8bn — outcomes do. Reframe or lose the second station.`,
                    coach_r2_output:        `<strong>More pages, same case.</strong> Restating the engineering scope in detail is still output thinking. The BCR is marginal because the benefits aren't articulated — describe the outcomes, not the works.`,
                    coach_r2_keep:          `<strong>You kept the value driver and made it real.</strong> The second station holds the catchment behind ~40% of benefits; integrating station-area development captures the regeneration value that lifts the BCR above the threshold.`,
                    coach_r2_defer:         `<strong>Deferring the second station is a hedge, not a plan.</strong> You protect the headline cost but push out the catchment that drives 40% of benefits — the BCR stays soft. If it drifts below 1.50 the station is cut for you.`,
                    coach_r2_cut:           `<strong>You've cut 40% of the benefits to save £620M.</strong> The BCR collapses, not improves — you removed the catchment that justified the scheme. That is descoping the value, not strengthening the case.`,
                    coach_r2_nobenefits:    `<strong>A benefit nobody owns is a benefit nobody realizes.</strong> Without funded benefits realization and a named owner after handover, your rebuilt BCR is a forecast that quietly never lands.`,
                    coach_r2_lowrigour:     `<strong>An asserted benefit is a benefit the reviewer discounts.</strong> A Treasury appraisal needs evidenced benefits — assert them and they'll be marked down, and your BCR with them. Evidence the case rigorously.`,
                    coach_r3_targetcost:    `<strong>Target-cost shares the cost risk and rewards beating the target.</strong> Pain/gain puts the supply chain's money where your schedule and cost goals are — the strongest alignment on the spectrum for a project this uncertain.`,
                    coach_r3_alliance:      `<strong>An alliance integrates the team around shared outcomes</strong> — a good instinct for a complex, interface-heavy project. It only works if the commercial model pays for outcomes, not activity, so don't leave the incentive at zero.`,
                    coach_r3_multipackage:  `<strong>Disaggregating lets you place each risk with the party best able to carry it.</strong> The specialist tunnellers take tunnel risk, the owner keeps what only the owner can manage — far better than one contractor pricing everything.`,
                    coach_r3_lumpsum:       `<strong>You've handed the whole £4.8bn and the tunnel to one lump-sum contractor.</strong> Either they price a £700M–1.1bn premium you can't afford, or they accept and go bust mid-tunnel — and the risk lands right back on you. The last scheme did exactly this and overran 60%.`,
                    coach_r3_riskright:     `<strong>That risk now sits with the party best able to manage it.</strong> A managed, surveyed shared pool on the tunnel and junction means the specialist carries what they control and the owner isn't blindsided — the central commercial lever, used well.`,
                    coach_r3_ownerall:      `<strong>Fully reimbursable leaves the cost risk entirely with you.</strong> The supply chain has no skin in the game on the tunnel, so there's no pressure to control cost. You've avoided the lump-sum premium but kept all the exposure.`,
                    coach_r3_dump:          `<strong>Dumping the tunnel risk and walking away is the lump-sum trap again.</strong> Risk transferred to a party that can't truly carry it doesn't disappear — it comes back as a premium, a dispute or a default. Manage it with them, don't offload it.`,
                    coach_r3_aligned:       `<strong>Model, risk allocation and incentive all aligned.</strong> A shared model on the tunnel with a real pain/gain incentive makes the supply chain <em>want</em> your cost and schedule — that's the difference between a structure and a slogan.`,
                    coach_r3_noincentive:   `<strong>An alliance without pain/gain is a friendlier way to overrun together.</strong> You picked an integrated model — good — but everyone's still paid for activity, not outcomes. What makes the supply chain want your targets?`,
                    coach_r3_underpremium:  `<strong>You chose a lump-sum but underpriced the risk premium.</strong> The supply chain will not carry £4.8bn of tunnel risk for a token premium — they'll either refuse, or accept and price the gap into claims later. Lump-sum risk is genuinely expensive.`,
                    coach_r4_gated:         `<strong>Staging the £4.8bn buys you optionality.</strong> Committing in tranches against design, enabling-works and main-works gates preserves the right to stop, reprice or descope as the tunnel uncertainty resolves — the single biggest cut to your worst-case exposure.`,
                    coach_r4_twostep:       `<strong>Two blocks is better than one cheque, but still coarse.</strong> Releasing almost everything at the start of construction surrenders most of your optionality just before the tunnel risk crystallizes. More, tighter gates protect you further.`,
                    coach_r4_full:          `<strong>You've authorized the whole budget before a single borehole.</strong> When the tunnel comes in £300M over, you have no gate to stop at and no leverage to reprice — you just write the cheque. What option did you give up by committing everything at once?`,
                    coach_r4_overcommit:    `<strong>Good gates, but you front-loaded the cash through them.</strong> Gates only protect you if the money waits behind them. Committing most of the £4.8bn early hands back the optionality the staging was meant to preserve.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>Hard go/no-go criteria make the gates real.</strong> Tunnel ground investigation and firm prices before the main-works release, with a retained right to descope, is what turns a gate from a formality into genuine control over your exposure.`,
                    coach_r4_schedule:      `<strong>A calendar gate isn't a gate.</strong> Releasing the next tranche on a date regardless of the evidence means the money flows whether or not the tunnel risk has resolved. Gate on evidence, not the diary.`,
                    coach_r4_nocriteria:    `<strong>Gates with no criteria are theatre.</strong> If the money always flows, you've staged nothing — you've kept the exposure of a full sanction with extra paperwork. Define what evidence unlocks each tranche.`,
                    coach_r4_nooptionality: `<strong>You've staged the cash but given up the right to act.</strong> Optionality is only worth something if you'll actually stop or reprice at a gate. Without that, the tranches are a payment schedule, not a control.`,
                    coach_r5_defined:       `<strong>Decision rights defined before the crisis.</strong> A sponsor board with clear stop/reprice/descope rights at each gate means the next shock is governed, not decided by whoever shouts loudest in the room.`,
                    coach_r5_committee:     `<strong>An advisory committee isn't decision rights.</strong> Non-binding monthly review feels like governance but holds no levers when the tunnel shock hits. Who actually decides to stop, reprice or push through?`,
                    coach_r5_crisis:        `<strong>Undefined rights get decided in the crisis.</strong> Leave it to whoever's in the room and the £300M shock is resolved by the loudest voice, not the structure. The prior scheme's overrun lived in exactly this gap.`,
                    coach_r5_absorb:        `<strong>The structure absorbed the shock.</strong> Repriced at a gate, contained in the shared risk pool, governed by a board with real rights — the £300M stayed in the contract and off the public balance sheet. That is owner-investing done right.`,
                    coach_r5_absorb_weak:   `<strong>You tried to absorb it, but the structure couldn't hold it.</strong> Without a shared risk pool and defined gate rights, “absorb at a gate” is a wish — most of the £300M still leaks onto the public balance sheet.`,
                    coach_r5_renegotiate:   `<strong>Re-opening the contract is the expensive path.</strong> Negotiating the £300M mid-delivery means delay and dispute, and you carry roughly half the shock anyway. A gate and a shared pool would have priced it without the fight.`,
                    coach_r5_balancesheet:  `<strong>The shock landed on the public balance sheet.</strong> Writing the £300M cheque is precisely the outcome the whole structure was meant to prevent — the owner absorbing risk the contract and gates should have contained.`,
                    coach_r5_fourkpi:       `<strong>A board-ready owner case.</strong> You defended BCR/NPV, schedule, cost variance and stakeholder value honestly, and showed the structure absorbing a live shock through the contract and gates. That is a funded scheme that survives.`,
                    coach_r5_costonly:      `<strong>Cost variance alone isn't the owner's story.</strong> Lead with the headline number and you invite the board to judge you as a builder. The owner's value is the BCR, the schedule, the risk allocation and the stakeholders together.`,
                    coach_r5_optimism:      `<strong>Leaving out the awkward numbers fools no one.</strong> A Treasury board has seen optimistic narratives before — the last scheme had one. Defend the real KPIs, including where you're exposed, or lose their confidence.`,

